<header class="artifact-hero">
  <div class="wrap">
    <div class="breadcrumb"><a href="/members/en/#biblioteca">Library</a> / Strength</div>
    <span class="label">Guide</span>
    <h1>Hypertrophy, strength and power</h1>
    <p>Three different adaptations, three different protocols, and a way to order them across the year without the gym wrecking your endurance training.</p>
  </div>
</header>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <p>The most common confusion in the gym is treating "getting strong" as one thing. It isn't. <strong>Making a muscle bigger, making it able to produce more force, and making it able to produce that force quickly are three different adaptations</strong>, and each one answers to a different stimulus.</p>

    <p>For a beginner they overlap: almost anything you do will make you bigger and stronger at the same time. As you advance they separate — and that is where choosing wrong costs you months.</p>

    <h2>The three, in one table</h2>

    <table class="tp-table tp-table--stack">
      <thead><tr><th>Adaptation</th><th>What triggers it</th><th>The protocol</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td class="tsb-band">Hypertrophy<br><span style="font-weight:400">size</span></td>
          <td data-label="What triggers it"><strong>Mechanical tension and volume.</strong> Heavy weight is not required: any load that leaves you between 5 and 30 reps builds muscle <em>if you go close to failure</em>. Effort is what governs it, not the number on the plate.</td>
          <td data-label="The protocol"><strong>6-12 reps</strong> · 60-80% · leaving <strong>1 to 3 reps in the tank</strong> · rest 90s-2min · the muscle you want to grow, stimulated <strong>every ~72 hours</strong>.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tsb-band">Max strength<br><span style="font-weight:400">how much</span></td>
          <td data-label="What triggers it"><strong>Heavy load — and it is a neurological skill</strong>, not a size. It is closer to learning a technical movement than to inflating a muscle: you can raise your strength a long way without gaining a gram.</td>
          <td data-label="The protocol"><strong>3-5 reps</strong> · 85% or more · <strong>3-5 sets</strong> · rest <strong>3-5 minutes</strong> · multi-joint movements with a barbell or dumbbells.</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td class="tsb-band">Power<br><span style="font-weight:400">how fast</span></td>
          <td data-label="What triggers it"><strong>Speed with maximal intent.</strong> Almost every athletic movement is resolved in 200-400 milliseconds: force you cannot apply within that window is no use to you.</td>
          <td data-label="The protocol"><strong>2-5 reps</strong> · 30-65% · rest <strong>3-5 minutes</strong> · <strong>if the bar slows down, the session is over</strong> — more sets only add fatigue.</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <p class="fineprint">You will find different rep ranges for hypertrophy depending on the source — 8-12, 8-15, 5-30. They contradict each other less than it looks: the wide range is real, and what decides is proximity to failure. The numbers above are the ones I use, so that it stays practical and repeatable.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Intent is part of the protocol, not a pep talk.</strong> In power work, even when the weight moves slowly, <em>trying</em> to move it as fast as possible is what produces the adaptation. Lifting the same weight "calmly" trains something else.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section--wash">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What this is worth if you run, ride or race triathlon</h2>

    <p>The gym does not make you more aerobic. What it does is more specific, and more valuable, than it is usually made out to be:</p>

    <ul>
      <li><strong>Economy.</strong> A stronger, stiffer neuromuscular system spends less energy at the same pace. It is the only way to go faster without asking anything more of the aerobic engine.</li>
      <li><strong>Injury resistance.</strong> Tendons, ligaments and connective tissue adapt more slowly than muscle, and far more slowly than the heart. Most overuse injuries show up because the tissue did not keep pace with the volume.</li>
      <li><strong>Durability.</strong> What breaks down in the third hour is not your VO2max: it is the ability to hold technique on tired legs.</li>
    </ul>

    <p><strong>What the gym does not do is hand you watts directly.</strong> That is a shortcut that gets repeated a lot, and it promises something that is not going to happen.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>The real problem: interference</h2>

    <p>Strength and endurance compete. Not as much as was claimed twenty years ago, but they compete — and for you, doing both in the same week, <strong>how you order them matters as much as what you do</strong>.</p>

    <table class="tp-table tp-table--stack">
      <thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>What to do</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td class="tsb-band">Same day</td><td data-label="What to do"><strong>Separate them by 6 hours or more</strong> if you can. If you can't, the endurance session goes first — it is your sport. The exception is a strength session that is the point of the day.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="tsb-band">Consecutive days</td><td data-label="What to do"><strong>Never heavy legs the day before a quality session or a long ride or run.</strong> It is the rule that gets broken most and the one that costs most.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="tsb-band">Where to put it</td><td data-label="What to do">Alongside a day that is already hard, rather than dirtying an easy day. Easy days have to stay easy: that is where the base gets built.</td></tr>
        <tr><td class="tsb-band">What interferes least</td><td data-label="What to do"><strong>Max strength.</strong> Low total volume, little local muscular fatigue. Hypertrophy interferes the most, precisely because high volume and muscle damage are the stimulus.</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <h2>How many days a week</h2>

    <p>You will read recommendations of <strong>3 to 5 gym days per week</strong>. It is a good number — for someone whose sport is the gym. With five hours of endurance on top it does not survive: fatigue, not time, is what limits you.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>Two sessions a week is the norm</strong> in my plans, and it is what most people sustain alongside endurance volume.</p>
      <p><strong>In a max-strength block you can go up to 3 or 4.</strong> It sounds contradictory and it isn't: max strength uses heavy load with very little total volume, so it accumulates little fatigue. It is a skill, and skills are practised often. What you cannot repeat four times a week is hypertrophy.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section--wash">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>How to order it across the year</h2>
    <p class="section-intro" style="text-align:left; margin-bottom:24px;">Pick your goal. The block changes a fair amount depending on which one it is — it is not the same periodization.</p>

    <div class="goal-switch">
      <div class="goal-switch-label">What are you after?</div>
      <div class="goal-opts">
        <button type="button" class="goal-opt" data-goal="perf" aria-pressed="true">Performance<span>go faster, without adding weight</span></button>
        <button type="button" class="goal-opt" data-goal="mass" aria-pressed="false">Muscle mass<span>I want to look stronger</span></button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ================= PATH A: performance ================= -->
    <div class="goal-panel" id="panel-perf">
      <h3>12-week block — performance</h3>
      <p>There is <strong>no hypertrophy phase</strong> here, and that is deliberate. Every kilo of new muscle is a kilo you carry up every climb and on every stride. What we are after is the neural adaptation — more force at the same body weight.</p>

      <table class="tp-table tp-table--stack">
        <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>In the gym</th><th>Outside</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="tsb-band">Weeks 1-4<br><span style="font-weight:400">Tolerance</span></td>
            <td data-label="In the gym"><strong>2×/week.</strong> 6-10 reps, moderate load, leaving 3 in the tank. <strong>The goal is not to grow: it is for tendon and connective tissue to tolerate what is coming</strong>, and to relearn the patterns. It feels easy and it is supposed to feel easy.</td>
            <td data-label="Outside">Aerobic base, high volume in z1-z2.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="tsb-band">Weeks 5-8<br><span style="font-weight:400">Max strength</span></td>
            <td data-label="In the gym"><strong>2×/week, up to 3-4 if the week allows it.</strong> 3-5 sets of 3-5 reps at 85%+, 3-5 minutes rest. Multi-joint: squat, deadlift, push, pull. <strong>This is the phase that will do the most for you.</strong></td>
            <td data-label="Outside">Volume stabilises and threshold intervals come in.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="tsb-band">Weeks 9-12<br><span style="font-weight:400">Power and sharpening</span></td>
            <td data-label="In the gym"><strong>2×/week dropping to 1.</strong> 2-5 reps at 30-65% with maximal intent: jumps, medicine-ball throws, fast bar. <strong>Hold the intensity and cut the volume</strong> — never the other way round.</td>
            <td data-label="Outside">Race pace, and the taper in the last 2 weeks.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <div class="callout">
        <p><strong>Each 4-week phase is 3 weeks of load and 1 easier</strong>, the same as your endurance blocks (2 and 1 if you are 50+). The 12-week block you will see published elsewhere runs 4+4+4 with no deload; that does not fit the real life of anyone who is also training outdoors.</p>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- ================= PATH B: mass ================= -->
    <div class="goal-panel" id="panel-mass" hidden>
      <h3>12-week block — muscle mass</h3>
      <p>It can be done. It just has to be a decision rather than an accident, and it has to land <strong>out of season</strong> — with no A race in the next four months.</p>

      <div class="cost-box">
        <strong>What it costs, said plainly:</strong> you are going to put on weight, and on climbs and on the run that is paid for in watts per kilo. You are going to carry more local muscular fatigue, which is the kind that interferes most with aerobic work. And you are going to have to <strong>eat for this</strong>: if you add gym volume without adding food, you don't grow — you just get tired. If your goal for the year is a time over a distance, this is not the route.
      </div>

      <table class="tp-table tp-table--stack">
        <thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>In the gym</th><th>Outside</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="tsb-band">Weeks 1-6<br><span style="font-weight:400">Hypertrophy</span></td>
            <td data-label="In the gym"><strong>3×/week.</strong> 6-12 reps leaving 1-2 in the tank, ~10-20 weekly sets per muscle group, and each muscle stimulated <strong>every ~72 hours</strong>. Machines and isolation work do belong here: they work, because all that matters is putting tension on the tissue.</td>
            <td data-label="Outside">Endurance in maintenance, mostly z2. This is not the moment for a hard block.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="tsb-band">Weeks 7-10<br><span style="font-weight:400">Max strength</span></td>
            <td data-label="In the gym"><strong>2-3×/week.</strong> 3-5 × 3-5 at 85%+, 3-5 minutes rest. <strong>This is where that new muscle becomes usable force</strong>; without this phase you ended up bigger and not stronger.</td>
            <td data-label="Outside">Intensity comes back, volume rises gradually.</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="tsb-band">Weeks 11-12<br><span style="font-weight:400">Power</span></td>
            <td data-label="In the gym"><strong>2×/week.</strong> 2-5 reps at 30-65%, maximal speed, and you stop when the bar slows down.</td>
            <td data-label="Outside">Back into your sport's specificity.</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>

      <p class="fineprint">If you are a beginner in the gym, the good news is that you don't have to choose yet: in the first months, what makes you bigger also makes you stronger. The split between protocols only starts to matter once you have been lifting seriously for a while.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section>
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>Maintaining, which is what you will be doing most of the time</h2>

    <p>For most of the year you are not building strength: you are trying not to lose it while endurance volume runs the calendar. The good news is that <strong>maintaining costs far less than building</strong>.</p>

    <div class="callout">
      <p><strong>One session a week is enough to hold what you gained</strong>, for several weeks, as long as you <strong>keep the load heavy and cut the volume</strong>. Two heavy sets of three reps do more to preserve your strength than four light sets of twelve.</p>
      <p>The classic race-week mistake is the reverse: dropping the weight and keeping the volume. That maintains nothing and leaves you tired on top of it.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>The four mistakes I see constantly</h2>
    <ol>
      <li><strong>Doing all three at once, every week.</strong> Sets of 10 with one-minute rests, something "heavy" at the end, and a few jumps. That is none of the three: it is cardio with weights.</li>
      <li><strong>Cutting the rest in max strength.</strong> Three minutes standing around feels ridiculous and is part of the exercise. If you rest 90 seconds, you are doing hypertrophy with not much weight.</li>
      <li><strong>Heavy legs the day before the quality session.</strong> The session that matters goes badly, and the conclusion drawn is "I'm out of shape".</li>
      <li><strong>Dropping the gym when the race block starts.</strong> Exactly when volume rises and the tissue needs it most.</li>
    </ol>

  </div>
</section>

<section class="section--wash">
  <div class="wrap">
    <h2>What to do with this</h2>
    <p>If you are one of my athletes, this is already handled in your plan: the phase you are in defines what shows up in TrainingPeaks, and the sessions come written. This guide is so you understand <strong>why</strong> your gym week changes with the time of year, and so you don't change it yourself at the worst possible moment.</p>
    <p>And if you want to do the mass block, let's talk about it first: <strong>what decides it is not whether you can, it is when</strong>. Write to me and we'll fit it into your calendar.</p>
  </div>
</section>

